          "type": "turn",
          "question": "If a clinician writes diabetes in one place and cataract in another, does the \"with\" convention connect them?",
          "answer": "The retrieved coding material does not answer this directly. None of the passages mention cataract, so I cannot say whether the \"with\" convention links diabetes and cataract.\n\nThe passages do describe how the convention works in general. The alphabetic index links two conditions with \"with,\" and the classification reads that as \"associated with\" or \"due to,\" presuming a relationship without the record stating it. This presumption applies only to pairings the index actually lists. It is overridden when the documentation says the two conditions are unrelated. The material I retrieved uses diabetic kidney disease as its example, not diabetes and cataract (*Coding type 2 diabetes: E11 and its complication subcategories*; *ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection*).\n\nThe passages also do not say how separately recorded diagnoses are handled. So I can't tell you whether location in the record matters.\n\nTo get a firm answer, a trained medical coder would need to check the current ICD-10-CM Alphabetic Index and the official coding guidelines for the relevant year. Those guidelines are updated each year.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
